WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.255 --> 00:00:07.424
My name's James from
Mountain Rug Cleaning.

00:00:07.424 --> 00:00:09.092
And today I'm going to be cleaning

00:00:09.092 --> 00:00:11.678
a rug infested with earthworms.

00:00:13.972 --> 00:00:15.849
The rug was just

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:19.728
completely saturated in mud, soaking wet.

00:00:19.728 --> 00:00:21.771
And then on further inspection,

00:00:21.771 --> 00:00:25.900
I could see what looked to be
little maggots inside the rug.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.863
Turns out it was probably
earthworm larvae.

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:32.615
It was a rug that somebody
didn't want anymore,

00:00:32.615 --> 00:00:36.244
so it was just discarded in their garden.

00:00:36.244 --> 00:00:39.664
Basically, I just wanted
to see if I could clean it,

00:00:39.664 --> 00:00:42.500
test my skills essentially,

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.836
and see what I could do with it.

00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:50.717
Essentially I'm just
rinsing the rug with water.

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:52.719
So it's just a high-powered hose,

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:55.388
and it's just pushing
water out onto the rug

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:59.017
to just rinse out as much
of the mud as possible.

00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:00.143
It's not a pressure washer.

00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:02.103
Pressure washer has a lot of power,

00:01:02.103 --> 00:01:04.189
but it doesn't necessarily shoot out

00:01:04.189 --> 00:01:06.149
a high volume of water onto the rug.

00:01:06.149 --> 00:01:07.984
And essentially what you
want to do in the beginning

00:01:07.984 --> 00:01:10.904
is just get the water on
there as quick as possible.

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:13.907
I have a pump attached to the hose

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:16.284
just so it gives enough
pressure and power.

00:01:16.284 --> 00:01:18.703
But it's not trickling out really slowly,

00:01:18.703 --> 00:01:19.996
it is powerful enough

00:01:19.996 --> 00:01:23.875
that you can sort of
blast the top of the rug

00:01:23.875 --> 00:01:25.460
and it will just sort of wash away

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:29.380
any sort of surface soils
from the top of the rug.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.133
You're going to be using
brushes on the rug,

00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:35.512
and the water alongside with the shampoo

00:01:35.512 --> 00:01:39.057
lubricates the rug to
prevent any kind of damage

00:01:39.057 --> 00:01:41.810
from the machines that you're
going to be using on it.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:45.396
All sorts of leaves and
twigs and soil and things

00:01:45.396 --> 00:01:46.856
are just blasting off the top of the rug,

00:01:46.856 --> 00:01:49.734
as well as the earthworms
that were in there.

00:01:49.734 --> 00:01:50.652
I wanted to make sure

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:53.404
that I got those washed
off the top of the rug,

00:01:53.404 --> 00:01:56.825
because I didn't want to churn
them all up into the rug.

00:01:56.825 --> 00:01:59.244
So I wanted to make sure that
I got those all off first

00:01:59.244 --> 00:02:00.745
before I took any more steps.

00:02:02.247 --> 00:02:04.624
I'm using just the watering can

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:07.544
with a mixture of shampoo

00:02:07.544 --> 00:02:10.130
and oxygen booster in it.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:11.548
And then I'm just using that

00:02:11.548 --> 00:02:15.343
to just get the shampoo distributed
over the top of the rug.

00:02:16.928 --> 00:02:18.847
So, that's the rotary machine.

00:02:18.847 --> 00:02:21.516
It has a rotating brush
on the bottom of it,

00:02:21.516 --> 00:02:24.727
and that is used to essentially

00:02:24.727 --> 00:02:27.856
get the shampoo mixed into the rug,

00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:30.984
so it's easier to then wash out.

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:33.862
I'm using a special fiber shampoo.

00:02:33.862 --> 00:02:36.614
It's quite a mild detergent,

00:02:36.614 --> 00:02:38.533
pet-safe, wool-safe.

00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:39.742
It's not very strong,

00:02:39.742 --> 00:02:42.370
it's just enough to break
down the soils in the rug

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:46.249
and make it easier for you
to sort of wash it all out.

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:47.542
The oxygen booster

00:02:47.542 --> 00:02:50.128
just helps break down
the soils a lot quicker.

00:02:50.128 --> 00:02:51.713
It can make things whiter,

00:02:51.713 --> 00:02:54.299
it can bring the colors out, reduce odor,

00:02:54.299 --> 00:02:55.758
and, yeah, just essentially

00:02:55.758 --> 00:02:58.344
make it easier for you
to remove the stains.

00:03:02.056 --> 00:03:03.725
I wouldn't use bleach at all.

00:03:03.725 --> 00:03:06.394
Bleach in general damages the fibers.

00:03:06.394 --> 00:03:08.354
Especially because this was a wool rug,

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:10.899
it can damage the colors in the rug.

00:03:10.899 --> 00:03:14.235
So it's better to just
avoid using the bleach.

00:03:22.452 --> 00:03:23.494
It's cold water.

00:03:23.494 --> 00:03:25.538
You don't want to use hot water on rugs,

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:29.751
because they tend to have
a problem with shrinking,

00:03:29.751 --> 00:03:32.045
so you just use cold water,

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:35.006
and that just eliminates any
kind of potential problems

00:03:35.006 --> 00:03:36.925
that you might have with cleaning them.

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:46.434
You then use what's called a squeegee,

00:03:46.434 --> 00:03:48.686
essentially like what you'd
use to clean your windows,

00:03:48.686 --> 00:03:51.522
and you scrape off all the dirt and foam

00:03:51.522 --> 00:03:52.732
from the top of the rug.

00:03:57.612 --> 00:03:59.739
The next step is flipping the rug over

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:02.784
and then cleaning the
wash floor underneath,

00:04:02.784 --> 00:04:04.244
'cause a lot of the dirt and stuff

00:04:04.244 --> 00:04:05.453
is still underneath the rug.

00:04:05.453 --> 00:04:08.915
So I think it's a good idea
to just wash all that away

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:10.833
before you then put the rug back on top

00:04:10.833 --> 00:04:12.377
that you've just cleaned.

00:04:12.377 --> 00:04:14.045
And then, again, it's pretty much

00:04:14.045 --> 00:04:16.589
exactly the same process
as doing the front.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:21.511
Just start off with getting
the rug saturated with water,

00:04:21.511 --> 00:04:24.847
adding the shampoo and
oxygen booster again,

00:04:24.847 --> 00:04:27.600
and then just cleaning the
back of it with the rotary

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.228
to get as many of the stains
out as you possibly can.

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:33.856
Always clean the back and the front,

00:04:33.856 --> 00:04:36.192
because when you clean the front

00:04:36.192 --> 00:04:39.279
it pushes a lot of the
dirt through into the back.

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:43.032
And then also on the flip
side, when you clean the back,

00:04:43.032 --> 00:04:46.619
it then forces a lot of the dirt and stuff

00:04:46.619 --> 00:04:49.956
which is deep in the rug
out from the opposite side,

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:51.165
if you see what I mean.

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:12.937
I usually then use a CRB machine

00:05:12.937 --> 00:05:14.439
on the top of the rug.

00:05:14.439 --> 00:05:16.107
It looks a bit like a vacuum.

00:05:16.107 --> 00:05:17.817
It's like a gray box.

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:20.445
It stands for counter-rotating brush head.

00:05:20.445 --> 00:05:22.322
Inside it's got two brushes

00:05:22.322 --> 00:05:25.074
that revolve in opposite directions.

00:05:25.074 --> 00:05:26.993
And then as they roll up,

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:29.203
they sort of pull the fibers of the rug

00:05:29.203 --> 00:05:30.455
up into the machine.

00:05:30.455 --> 00:05:33.791
So it kind of gives you
a much deeper clean,

00:05:33.791 --> 00:05:36.002
but it also lifts the pile of the rug,

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:37.754
making it a bit more fluffy.

00:05:42.091 --> 00:05:44.260
I'm adding more products to the rug

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.596
to sort of break down more soils

00:05:46.596 --> 00:05:48.348
that are still left inside.

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:52.018
You do it until the water
comes out as clear as possible.

00:05:52.018 --> 00:05:54.854
You'll never get a rug 100% clean.

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:58.066
There will always be
some dirt inside a rug.

00:05:58.066 --> 00:06:00.234
You could be using thousands

00:06:00.234 --> 00:06:01.652
and thousands of liters of water

00:06:01.652 --> 00:06:04.572
unnecessarily for very, very little gain.

00:06:04.572 --> 00:06:06.115
So there's a point at which

00:06:06.115 --> 00:06:08.409
it's still getting little bits out,

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:11.287
but it's just not worth
wasting so much water

00:06:11.287 --> 00:06:12.789
in order to get it out.

00:06:13.915 --> 00:06:17.335
At that point, I'm adding
acidic rinse to the rug.

00:06:17.335 --> 00:06:20.338
It neutralizes all the chemicals

00:06:20.338 --> 00:06:21.881
that you've previously used,

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.133
and it helps to wash them all out.

00:06:24.133 --> 00:06:27.845
It also helps prevent cellulosic browning,

00:06:27.845 --> 00:06:30.640
which is a process at
which the natural fibers

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.725
in the rug can turn brown.

00:06:34.143 --> 00:06:36.104
That is called a Grandi Groom.

00:06:36.104 --> 00:06:38.564
It's used to remove the
pattern left in the rug,

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:42.360
but it's also to set the
fibers to help it dry quicker.

00:06:44.112 --> 00:06:47.740
I'm vacuuming out as much
moisture as I possibly can.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:50.076
So that's basically a wet vac,

00:06:50.076 --> 00:06:52.370
and it's sucking out the moisture

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:55.289
and then it's going into a tank.

00:06:55.289 --> 00:06:57.125
It's quite an important process,

00:06:57.125 --> 00:06:58.876
'cause it is obviously removing

00:06:58.876 --> 00:07:00.962
a lot of the moisture from the rug.

00:07:00.962 --> 00:07:02.588
Then I put it in a centrifuge,

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:06.175
which gets about 95% of
the water out of the rug.

00:07:06.175 --> 00:07:10.012
You hang the rug in a warm room

00:07:10.012 --> 00:07:12.181
with fans and a dehumidifier.

00:07:12.181 --> 00:07:14.475
And then that just gets
the last remaining bits

00:07:14.475 --> 00:07:16.018
of the moisture out.

00:07:18.354 --> 00:07:21.190
I mean, don't leave
your rug in the garden,

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:22.567
for a start.

00:07:23.818 --> 00:07:25.486
But, yeah, the biggest tip I've got

00:07:25.486 --> 00:07:29.031
for taking care of your
rug is lots of vacuuming.

00:07:29.031 --> 00:07:31.242
The worst thing that damages a rug

00:07:31.242 --> 00:07:32.910
is probably the dry soils

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.371
that are in the rug for
long periods of time.

00:07:35.371 --> 00:07:37.457
It kind of can wear down the rug.

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:38.791
So lots of vacuuming and regular beating.

00:07:41.752 --> 00:07:43.254
I didn’t really feel comfortable 

00:07:43.254 --> 00:07:46.757
with then giving it on to somebody else,

00:07:46.757 --> 00:07:48.885
so after I cleaned it, rather than just

00:07:48.885 --> 00:07:50.470
throwing it in the bin and wasting it,

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:51.679
I thought a good thing to do

00:07:51.679 --> 00:07:53.389
would be to give it to a dog shelter.

00:07:53.389 --> 00:07:54.474
So that’s what I did.

